  "Question": "Of 10 products, 4 are defective. If 3 are drawn without replacement, what is the probability that exactly 1 is defective?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "1/2",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "$C(4,1)\\times C(6,2)/C(10,3)=4\\times15/120=1/2$.\n\nAlternative method 1: Separate the three possible draw orders\n\nUnder the recorded interpretation of uniform random sampling, exactly one defective item appears in one of three disjoint orders: DGG, GDG, or GGD, where D means defective and G means nondefective. For each order, the pool sizes are \\(10,9,8\\), and the applicable category counts contribute \\(4,6,5\\) in the appropriate order. Thus each order has probability\n\\[\n\\frac{4\\cdot6\\cdot5}{10\\cdot9\\cdot8}=\\frac16.\n\\]\nThese three orders cover every draw with exactly one defective item and never overlap. Therefore the required probability is \\(3\\cdot\\frac16=\\frac12\\).",
